L3 systems (conditional driving automation) further allow drivers to look away from the driving environment. However, in both cases, drivers are still responsible to take over control from automated driving, should the systems reach their limits. This drawback in L2 and L3 sys­ tems raises a major safety challenge that the removal of drivers’ involvement in the perceptual-motor control loop (Mole et al., 2019) during automation may impair their perception and comprehension of the environment (Goncalves et al., 2020), which might ultimately compromise their take-over driving performance (Eriksson & Stanton, 2017; Gold et al., 2016; Li et al., 2021). As drivers heavily rely on visual information to support motor control of driving, knowing drivers’ gaze strategies in the context of automation, especially during the transition of control, enhances our ability to tackle this challenge.
Existing empirical studies have revealed that automation affects gaze behaviours (Feldhütter et al., 2017; Louw et al., 2017; Louw & Merat, 2017; Mackenzie & Harris, 2015; Schnebelen et al., 2019). Here, reduced visual attention towards the road center is seen with an increase in automation level (Carsten et al., 2012; Louw & Merat, 2017), which is sometimes exacerbated by increased engagement with visual nondriving related tasks (Huisingh et al., 2015; Louw et al., 2019; Zeeb et al., 2016). Drivers also tend to look further ahead in the road scene (Mackenzie & Harris, 2015) and scan more horizontally (Dambo¨ck et al., 2013; Louw & Merat, 2017) when they were not in active physical control of the vehicle. Such erratic gaze patterns and inattention to the road scene, diminishing drivers’ situation awareness (Endsley and Kaber, 1999), have been demonstrated to cause more incorrect takeover responses with a higher collision rate (Louw et al., 2017; Zeeb et al., 2015). However, Feldhütter et al. (2017) revealed that a long period of automation and engagement with non-driving related tasks led to less cumulative gaze duration towards the road scene and longer re­ action time, but no significant changes in terms of accelerations and time to collision. These controversial findings in existing studies regarding the link between changes in drivers’ gaze patterns and the take-over performance might be attributed to Feldhütter et al. (2017)’s use of a notable longer time budget (6 s) as compared to Louw et al. (2017) (3 s) and Zeeb et al. (2015) (2.5–3.5 s). However, there is a lack in literature to address this postulation by exploring changes in drivers’ gaze patterns during the transition period, under varying time budgets.
Louw et al. (2019) also investigated how drivers divided their attention across different areas of interest (AoIs) not only during the automation but also during the transition of control, with or without an additional non-driving related task (visual arrow task). It adds new in­ sights to literature by showing that the human–machine interface (HMI, integrated into the instrument cluster) displaying the status of the sys­ tem occupied quite a proportion of attention during automation, and drivers further prioritized the HMI over the road scene for perception in the early stage of control transitions, regardless of whether they were performing the additional non-driving related task. This implies that more considerations should be devoted to ensuring that drivers’ intui­ tive visual sampling from the HMI would not interfere with the detection of on-road incidents. A potential approach to diminish the issue of impaired situation awareness and the visual competence between the road scene and the HMI, in this context, might be head-up displays (HUDs) (Dambo¨ck et al., 2012; Feierle et al., 2019; Scho¨mig et al., 2018). Conventional HUDs in vehicles project speedometer, tachometer, and other driving-related information to the windshield or somewhere ahead in the field of view, while more informative prototypes further overlay animations onto the surroundings in the form of augmented reality (Feierle et al., 2019). While HUDs have been proven to improve driving performance and cause less mental stress in manual driving (Liu, 2003; Liu & Wen, 2004), they also show merits of, in the context of automated driving, keeping drivers from looking away from the road scene when searching for information that would otherwise be pre­ sented on the instrument cluster (Scho¨mig et al., 2018).
However, solely retaining drivers’ attention on the road might not be sufficient, as gaze should also be well coordinated with motor control to

