ensure safe driving (Mole et al., 2019). Research from manual driving (Kountouriotis et al., 2012; Land & Lee, 1994; Lappi et al., 2013; Leh­ tonen et al., 2014) has shown that drivers tend to execute overt stimulus sampling in a “move-dwell-move” manner by executing “guiding fixa­ tions” (GFs; on-road fixations allocated around 1–2 s time headway ahead of the ego car), and intermittently scanning even further ahead with interleaving “look-ahead fixations” (LAFs; fixations previewing considerably further ahead on the road than where GFs are directed). Literature generally suggests that GFs help determine impending motor commands for positional control in a short timescale, while LAFs serve for future hazard anticipation and trajectory planning (Lappi & Mole, 2018; Lehtonen et al., 2013, 2014; Mars et al., 2012; Mole et al., 2019; Wilkie et al., 2008). Such gaze strategy of alternating between GFs and LAFs, referred to as gaze polling behaviour, was also addressed in the circumstance of automated driving (Mars et al., 2012; Navarro et al., 2016; Schnebelen et al., 2019), which showed that drivers tend to executed fewer GFs and more LAFs (compared with manual driving). It was also found in Navarro et al., 2016 that even if drivers had kept their attention on the road and noticed the obstacle at similar times (so had a similar time budget to evade), they performed evasive maneuvers more aggressively in take-over driving, as compared to manual driving. Mole et al. (2019) attributed these observations to the decoupled gaze mode from steering caused by automation and inferred that there should be a recoupling period in the early stage of control resumptions, regardless of whether drivers were looking at the road upon receiving take-over re­ quests. Through characterizing the dynamics of drivers’ gaze polling behaviours, one can shed more light on the gaze recoupling process of how drivers altered their visual strategies dynamically to support physical control, which might be of extra significance to road safety under varying takeover urgency (manipulated by the length of the time budget). However, few attempts in literature have addressed this.
1.1. Gaze control theory
To better understand the underlying causes of the changes in drivers’ gaze strategies, it is necessary to borrow some knowledge from the field of human gaze control. Existing literature generally interprets human’s overt shift of attention from two dichotomous aspects: (1) the bottom-up orientation driven by the properties of stimuli (e.g., saliency) or (2) the top-down orientation controlled according to the prior knowledge or task requirements (Henderson, 2003; Shiferaw et al., 2019). A growing body of literature agrees on a theoretical integration of gaze control as a prediction process that reduces the error based on the external (bottomup) information and internal (top-down) expectation, through selective visual sampling (Adams et al., 2013; Rauss & Pourtois, 2013; Shiferaw et al., 2019). In this context, to selectively sample information from the redundant visual world, the brain prioritizes the encoding of less frequent, but more salient, features by suppressing frequently occurring ones (Koch & Poggio, 1999; Shiferaw et al., 2019). In the absence of topdown modulation, locations with salient features are more likely to be fixated through the bottom-up influence. Meanwhile, the brain makes top-down predictions about the relevant incoming perceptual informa­ tion to mediate oculomotor actions along with the bottom-up influence. These predictions originate from observers’ internal state, e.g., in­ tentions, and prior knowledge of the scene and task requirements. As observers’ internal state can also be influenced by external information via the ascending bottom-up process, which, in turn, will also influence the bottom-up process through top-down modulation, the bottom-up and top-down processes are not mutually exclusive but closely interde­ pendent (Shiferaw et al., 2019). These underpinnings of human gaze control have been incorporated into a conceptual gaze control model formulated by Shiferaw et al. (2019), which provides a more theoretical perspective to interpret drivers’ gaze patterns in the circumstance of automated driving and control transitions. However, to the best of the authors’ knowledge, no empirical studies on driving gaze behaviours have attempted to explain observations with these gaze control

