1.2. Current study
To fill the aforementioned research gaps, the current study aimed to provide a deeper understanding of changes in drivers’ gaze patterns throughout the entire time domain around the transition of control (including both phases before and after and TORs), with variations in the automation level (L2 and L3) and time budget (2 s and 6 s). A HUD, positioned above the road path, was used to relay information about the automation system and indicate TORs, which aimed to encourage drivers to maintain their attention on the road. We expanded an existing conceptual framework of gaze control to reflect the circumstance of take-over driving, based on which theoretical explanations for the observed gaze patterns were formulated. In this paper, it was hypothe­ sized that the effects of the automation level on drivers’ gaze patterns during automation would carry over to the subsequent take-over phase, which should also vary with the length of time budget.
The rest of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 presents the details of experiment conduction and data collection. Section 3 describes the procedure of data processing as well as the statistical methods used in this study. Section 4 illustrates experimental results, and this is fol­ lowed by Section 5, where the effects of the automation level, time budget, and HUD on drivers’ gaze patterns were discussed based on the conceptual gaze control model. Finally, key conclusions, implications, and limitations of this study are summarized in Section 6.
2. Methods
2.1. Apparatus
The experiments were conducted using the motion-based driving simulator of the State Key Laboratory of Automotive Safety and Energy at Tsinghua University, China (see Fig. 1). A full-scale BMW series-3 mock cab with all driving control operations is mounted on a 6-de­ gree-of-freedom motion base. The projection system comprises 5 screens, providing a frontal field of view of approximately 200◦ × 50◦ (horizontal × vertical) and a rearward field of view of 50◦ × 30◦ from driver’s perspective. All manual operational inputs, vehicle’s dynamic status data, and its spatial positions in the simulated scenarios were recorded at a frequency of 60 Hz. SensoMotoric Instruments Eye Tracking Glasses 2 wireless (SMI ETG) were used to collect drivers’ eye

