movements, including raw gaze position, pupil size, and gaze vectors, at a sampling rate of 60 Hz, along with the frontal-view stimuli recorded at 30 Hz and 960 × 710 pixels. Physiological data, including electrodermal activity (EDA), electromyogram (EMG) and photoplethysmographic signals (PPG) were collected by the BIOPAC MP150 system at 1000 Hz. Subjective workload assessment data were also collected using the NASA Task Load Index (NASA-TLX, Hart & Staveland, 1988) questionnaire.
2.2. Participants
Following approval by the Tsinghua University Ethics Committee, 37 participants were recruited via the internet and advertising posters in Beijing, China. 11 participants were excluded from the analysis due to either simulator motion sickness (3 participants) or unreliable data collection (8 participants, participants closed their eyes for a while or/ and adjusted the eye-tracking glasses during driving yielding uncali­ brated gaze coordinates). The remaining 26 participants (6 females and 20 males) included 11 young (23–27 years old), 13 middle-aged (28–50 years old), and 2 old (>50 years old) drivers. Each held a valid driving license for at least 2 years (Mean = 7.04, SD = 5.30). The proportions for the short (5,000–10,000 km), medium (10,000–100,000 km), and long (>100,000 km) driving mileage were 34.6 % (n = 9), 46.2 % (n = 12), and 19.2 % (n = 5), respectively. Drivers were compensated 120 RMB for taking part.
2.3. Human-machine interface and take-over request
A head-up display was used to provide information about the auto­ mation status (see Fig. 2). During automation, a green steering wheel symbol superimposed by a WI-FI icon was shown in the central-upper region in the frontal field of view. A text message was also included beneath the symbol indicating that the L2/L3 automation was activated (in Chinese). A visual-auditory TOR was employed in this study. When it came to situations that required drivers to respond, the HMI changed accordingly (see Fig. 2d-e). Additionally, the interface flashed at a rate of 1 Hz and was accompanied by high-frequency auditory warning beeps.
