2.5. Operating the automated driving system
In this study, the automation function was implemented using an MPC-based vehicle path-tracking algorithm (Li et al., 2017). The auto­ mation system was activated automatically when the vehicle entered its operational design domain (SAE International, 2018), keeping the vehicle driving in the center lane at 90 km/h. Upon activation, a voice message was broadcast (in Chinese) indicating that the automation was engaged. At the same time, the HUD displaying the L2/L3 automation symbol was shown to drivers. At this stage, participants were no longer responsible for the dynamic driving tasks and were allowed to move their feet and hands off the pedals and steering wheel, respectively. For L2, participants were required to monitor the environment during automation. While for L3, after around 20-s activation of the automa­ tion, a voice message announced the beginning of the SuRT (in Chinese). Participants then performed the SuRT and were not required to monitor the road.
Take-over events in this study occurred when the ego car was about to exit the operational design domain of the system, i.e., exiting the highway and entering a straight or right-turn off-ramp, as these events account for most of the situations in practice (Eriksson & Stanton, 2017; SAE International, 2018). When exiting the operational design domain, the automation system would disengage, and a visual-auditory TOR was issued. Participants were required to resume the control of the vehicle, both laterally and longitudinally, by steering, braking, or accelerating. The visual-auditory TOR was dismissed once participants gave any effective manual operational inputs, i.e., a steering input >5 degrees, a brake pressure reaching 0.5 MPa, or the ego car’s acceleration exceeding 0.001 m/s2. To avoid unexpected driver-initiated take-over operations, participants could not override the automation system unless a TOR was given.

2.6. Experimental and scenario design
We used a 2 × 2 × 2 within-participant design, with three inde­ pendent variables: time budget (TB; 2 s, 6 s), automation level (L2, L3), and roadway curvature (straight, curved). As shown in Fig. 3, each participant completed a training-manual drive and four automated drives, which lasted about 100 min, altogether. The training-manual drive contained two sessions, i.e., the training session and the manual session. During the training session, participants practiced how to con­ trol the vehicle, perform the SuRT, and resume control from automation. This was followed by the manual session, during which drivers were asked to drive the car manually and data were recorded as the baseline for comparison with L2 and L3 conditions. As shown in Fig. 4, drives for L2 or L3 condition comprised four take-over trials, two for straight takeovers and two for curved ones, which occurred in an interleaving sequence. After each trial, the simulation was paused, followed by a NASA-TLX questionnaire inquiring subjective workload of the take-over trial just experienced. Then, the next trial followed. The driving sce­ narios for these five drives were similar, but the starting and ending points as well as the surrounding layouts of the road varied, to reduce participants’ familiarity with the scenarios.
The highway comprised two normal driving lanes (3.5 m in width), and one emergency lane (3 m in width), in both directions. To minimize drivers’ familiarity with the scenarios, the road included a mixture of curved and straight segments. Randomized off-road objects, including buildings, bridges, trees and mountains, were also incorporated. The speed range for manual driving, including the take-over periods, was 80–120 km/h, though drivers were encouraged to drive at around 90 km/h if they felt safe to do so. All TORs were prompted during the straight-approaching section before entering the off-ramps with a designed time budget (2 s, 6 s) (see Fig. 5). Participants were required to turn right or not, indicated by the layout of the traffic cones placed in the blocked direction. As road traffic can lead to highly road-eventdependent gaze patterns (Goncalves et al., 2020), which might inter­ fere with the influences of the primary factors (automation level and time budget) of the current study, no ambient traffic was included in the driving environment.
