Time-series representation of gaze distribution, the distribution of drivers’ first fixation location after TORs, the proportions of gaze time towards AoIs, gaze time and frequency of GFs and LAFs, and gaze polling frequency were used to assess how drivers allocate their attention during different phases around transitions.
The proportions of gaze time towards different AoIs (during the specific time windows of interest) were statistically analyzed using IBM SPSS Statistics 25. Kolmogorov-Smirnov and Shapiro-Wilk tests showed that not all measurements were normally distributed, even after trans­ formations. Thus, the nonparametric Friedman test and Wilcoxon signed-rank test were used in statistical analysis. The Friedman test is a statistical test used to determine whether 3 or more measurements from the same group of participants significantly differ from each other. It is a non-parametric alternative to the commonly used one-way ANOVA (analysis of variance) with repeated measures when the assumption of the normal distribution is violated. Similarly, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the nonparametric test equivalent to the paired-sample t-test that was used to compare two sets of measurements from the same partici­ pants, while it also does not require measurements to meet the normal distribution assumption. In this study, Friedman tests were conducted separately for each time budget condition, to determine the simple main effects of the automation level (as the within-participant factor). Posthoc pairwise comparisons were conducted using Wilcoxon signed-rank

