5.2. Automation level and gaze patterns before TORs
Findings showed that drivers’ gaze patterns during automation differed significantly from manual driving. During L3 automation, drivers mainly focused on the SuRT, as they were told that there was no need for monitoring the environment in this case. A more important finding was that when relinquishing physical control to L2 automation, drivers redirected their gaze from the GF area (Near Road) to the LAF area (Far Road), Speedometer, HUD, as well as other areas beyond the road (compared with manual driving). The increase in attention towards non-road areas during L2 automation was in line with existing literature (Louw et al., 2019; Louw & Merat, 2017). Moreover, the diversion of attention from the GF area to the LAF area caused by vehicle automation in straight road driving has also been observed by studies concerning driving in a bend (Mackenzie & Harris, 2015; Schnebelen et al., 2019). However, in this study, much lower gaze polling frequency was observed in L2 automation compared with manual driving, which differed from

the observations in Schnebelen et al., 2019. This should be attributed to the variations in vehicle kinematic motions between driving on straight roads and curved roads, which, as Mackenzie & Harris (2015) claimed, affected the placement of drivers’ vision.
The distinctions between L2 and manual driving in our experiments could be due to the difference in task requirements. Unlike manual driving, drivers’ primary task in L2 automation switched from active lane-keeping and road event tackling to passive monitoring (switching from S4 to S1 in Fig. 16). Thus, the top-down modulation intended for active gaze-steering coordination was weakened, and the priority for GFs closely serving for ongoing positional control (Lappi & Mole, 2018) was inherently lowered. In the meantime, the top-down commands, reflecting the need for environmental monitoring, albeit mild, encour­ aged drivers to redistribute more gazes to the LAF area, for hazard detection/anticipation, as well as to the HUD and Speedometer, for system status checking. Concomitantly, the bottom-up influence was also enhanced, since the symbolic HUD concept used here was quite salient compared to the background of the environment, even in the peripheral view. In this way, the integrated effects of both top-down and bottom-up information in L2 not only released drivers’ attention from the near roadway (GFs) but also guided their attention to the HUD when drivers were looking forward. These implications demonstrate that the layout of our HUD concept has the potential to maintain drivers’ attention on the road for road-event detection, which is of extra signif­ icance to road safety in the case of take-overs in L2 driving.
5.3. Automation level and gaze patterns immediately after TORs
The preference for the AoIs to be fixated first when receiving TORs

