should be dominated by a different gaze control mechanism, specif­ ically, ascribed to the different levels of situation awareness in L2 and L3 take-overs. During L2 automation, as highlighted above, drivers made more glances to the central road area (mostly covering the LAF area and the HUD). Once a TOR was issued, the HUD altered from a constant green symbol to a twinkling red one, which increased the saliency of the HUD and would attract drivers’ attention through the bottom-up influ­ ence of gaze control. In the meantime, suffering from the delay due to cognitive processing, new top-down cues reflecting the comprehension of the unexpected TOR (both visual and auditory) and changes of drivers’ primary task (switching from monitoring to gaze-steering recoupling, then to routine driving) would thrive later than the bottom-up. Thus, the top-down influence remained to be originated based on previous temporal comprehension of the scene (situation awareness). On this basis, drivers’ preference for the first fixation in L2 take-overs mainly reflected the changes in bottom-up inputs, while the changes in top-down influence emitted its influence in subsequent gaze behaviours. However, these implications should be underpinned by two assumptions (1) the incoming scenery information was not salient enough to compete with the TOR symbol and (2) drivers had not pre­ dicted the incoming TOR based on historical situation awareness. This can also be re-affirmed from Fig. 11 where the preference for the HUD and Far Road (LAF area) regarding the first fixation location was less prominent given a shorter time budget in L2 take-overs. Because in this case (L2, TB = 2 s), the off-ramp and traffic cones were more visible around the onset of TORs, hence, some drivers might have already anticipated the imminent TOR in advance (due to the learning effect after multiple trails), and they altered top-down commands accordingly.
Regarding drivers’ first fixation distribution in L3, the dominant priority of attention towards the GF area was mainly driven by the topdown orientation. Once drivers heard the auditory TORs, they would have realized something happened but failed to predict the nature of the unfolding event (due to reduced situation awareness in L3) until they switched their attention back to the road. In this circumstance, a safer visual strategy was to dedicate early glances towards the road path in short time headway (GF area), as a potential hazard in this area would be more dangerous to the ego car and GFs could support ongoing posi­ tional control timely to minimize the danger. Thus, the top-down com­ mands reflecting this gaze strategy based on drivers’ prior knowledge/ experience, would dominantly guide their first fixation towards the GF area.
In Louw et al., 2019, it was revealed that there was a visual competence between the road scene and the instrument-cluster-based HMI immediately after automation failure. Drivers in Louw et al., 2019 prioritized the HMI to the road scene for information perception, in the early stage of take-overs, which raised the concern that the perception from the HMI might impede drivers from looking back to the road timely. However, in the current study, the HUD concept partially undertook the function of the instrument-cluster-based HMI by relaying information of the automation system’s status. Thus, the top-down in­ fluence reflecting this prior knowledge lowered the demand for checking with the Speedometer but increased the priority for road scene and HUD perception. Correspondingly, since the HUD could be easily observed when looking forward even using peripheral view, more attention to the roadway and much less to the Speedometer (the instrument cluster) were found as compared to observations in Louw et al., 2019, during the crucial early stage after TORs (the first 2-s period). This implication is of greater significance for L3 take-over driving where drivers had muchreduced situation awareness in the beginning.
5.4. Time budget and gaze polling patterns after TORs
The impact of time budget on drivers’ gaze patterns was different for L2 and L3 take-overs. For L2 take-overs, the time budget mainly made a difference on gaze polling patterns in the period roughly between the TOR and the Cutting-point, where drivers gradually diverted their

