glances from the LAF area to the GF area. While in L3, drivers prioritized attention towards the GF area, with a gradual increase in attention to the LAF area in the early stage of control transition, which was then fol­ lowed by distinctive evolving trends for different time budget condi­ tions. These different trends of the trade-off between GFs and LAFs indicated that the gaze recoupling process (Mole et al., 2019) was influenced by the interaction between the take-over urgency (manipu­ lated by the time budget) and drivers’ prior situation awareness (manipulated by the automation level).
As the ego car approached the off-ramps, three aspects contributed to the need for more GFs in both L2 and L3 take-overs: (1) the task requirement for gaze-steering recoupling in the early stage of take-overs, (2) the approaching off-ramps and traffic ones, and (3) higher pressure demanding for smooth lane-keeping before entering the hazard zone (the area between Cut-off point and Leaving point in Fig. 6). Taking the L2 take-over with 6 s of time budget for instance, initially, because drivers had decoupled gaze from steering during automation (fewer GFs and more LAFs as compared to manual driving), the top-down influence reflecting changes in task requirements (switching from S1 to S3 in Fig. 16) encouraged more GFs to guide ongoing positional control. Af­ terwards, as the off-ramp and traffic cones approached and became more visible and salient, an increasing proportion of glances could be attracted through the influence of top-down (driven by the need for road event tackling) and bottom-up (driven by the distinguishing saliency of the road event) interaction. This proportion of attention dedicated to the on-road event was mainly merged into the category of LAFs when the off-ramps and traffic cones still lay in the LAF area from driver’s perspective and then regarded as GFs when the ego car continued to move ahead. Besides, the closer the off-ramp was, the higher pressure it applied to drivers for lane-keeping, which also prioritized the (topdown) demand for motor control and resulted in more GFs. These three processes took place sequentially when given a long time budget of 6 s, which caused the gradual attentional diversion from LAFs to GFs to be almost proportional with the lapse of time. Similarly, given a shorter time budget of 2 s, these three processes partially overlapped in time­ scale, which made the time-proportional diversion from LAFs to GFs to be scaled with the length of time budget (L2 curves in Fig. 13a).
However, for the L3 situation, due to lack of situation awareness, the task requirement for gaze-steering recoupling in the early stage of takeovers required drivers to perform both more GFs (for ongoing positional control) and LAFs (for further hazard anticipating or detecting), whereby the situation awareness level also increased. Given the time budget of 6 s, this recoupling process could be distinguished from Fig. 13b and Fig. 14b (blue curves) where the GF curves reached the first maximal (point A in Fig. 13b and Fig. 14b) about 1 s after the TOR (for TOR1 in Fig. 13b and Fig. 14b), and then decreased until around 3.5 s after the TOR (for TOR1 in Fig. 13b and Fig. 14b) with the LAF curves continuously rising to the first maximal (point B in Fig. 13b and Fig. 14b). Afterwards, the attention diversion from LAFs to GFs took place and shaped in a similar trend as that in L2 take-overs (see the trend of curves after point B in Fig. 13c and Fig. 14c), which was also caused by the latter two aspects contributing to the increase of GFs (discussed in the immediately preceding paragraph). While given a shorter time budget of 2 s, both GFs and LAFs competed for attention in a limited time budget. Thus, drivers prioritized GFs ahead of LAFs when approaching the off-ramps and traffic cones, which led to much fewer LAFs than other take-over conditions even after the ego car had left the Leaving point for quite a while (see orange curves in Fig. 13b and Fig. 14b).
Thus, based on preceding discussions, it can be implied that a time budget of 6 s was long enough to enable drivers in L3 to gain as good situation awareness as that in L2 take-over driving in our scenarios. However, cautions must be applied when interpreting the time for sit­ uation awareness rebuilding with the specific value, as this might be highly dependent upon the complexity of the scenario and the length of time budget (Gold et al., 2013, 2016, 2017). Furthermore, when developing the automation systems, the minimum leading time for

