6. Conclusion
This driving simulator study aimed to investigate drivers’ gaze pat­ terns around the transition of control, under varying automation levels and time urgency, where a HUD concept located above the roadway was introduced as an HMI to provide TOR messages. A conceptual gaze control model from literature (Shiferaw et al., 2019), based on the interaction of top-down and bottom-up influence, was extended to theoretically explain the observations of drivers’ gaze patterns in takeover driving.
Findings confirmed the previous observations (Louw et al., 2019; Louw & Merat, 2017) that L2 automation elicited more voluntary glances beyond the road scene as the top-down modulation for active gaze-steering coordination was weakened when drivers’ primary task changed to passive monitoring, and engaging with non-driving related tasks led to further reduced attention dedicated to the roadway. How­ ever, the driving risk caused by such decoupled gaze patterns might be mitigated by the proposed HUD concept in this study, as it showed the potential to maintain drivers’ attention to the road center during auto­ mated driving and encourage drivers to glance the road earlier by reducing glances to the instrument cluster immediately after TORs. On straight roads, drivers executed similar gaze polling behaviours of negotiating between GFs and LAFs as those reported in curved-road sections (Mars et al., 2012; Schnebelen et al., 2019), and more LAFs were observed during L2 automation (Schnebelen et al., 2019).
Findings also confirmed the hypothesis that the effects of the auto­ mation level on drivers’ gaze patterns during the automation phase carried over to the subsequent take-over phase, which also varied with the length of the time budget. Specifically, after TORs, drivers priori­ tized their attention, firstly towards different areas for different auto­ mation levels, i.e., largely remained on the HUD and LAF area (Far Road) in L2, while tremendously dedicated to the GF area (Near Road) in L3. This was driven by different gaze control mechanisms depending on drivers’ prior situation awareness during automation. And this carry­ over effect resulting from where drivers were looking before TORs also influenced drivers’ subsequent gaze polling patterns in different time­ scales, which was also modulated by the length of time budget. For L2 take-overs, the time budget for responses mainly made a difference on gaze polling patterns roughly within the period of the time budget, where drivers gradually diverted their glances from the LAF area to the GF area. And this attentional diversion changed nearly in proportion to the lapse of time. While in L3 situations, drivers’ attentional distribution was similar in the early stage of take-overs for both time budget con­ ditions (2 s vs. 6 s). They prioritized their early glances to the GF area with a gradual increase in attention towards the LAF area, which then was followed by distinctive evolving trends in different time budget conditions. In our experimental scenarios, a time budget of 6 s was long enough to enable drivers in L3 to regain as good situation awareness as that in L2 take-over driving. While given a shorter time budget of 2 s, both GFs and LAFs would compete for attention throughout the period within time budget in L3 take-overs, and drivers prioritized GFs ahead of LAFs at this stage, which might lead to insufficient hazard detection/ anticipation from far roadway ahead.
By interpreting drivers’ gaze patterns from the perspective of gaze control and gaze-steering coordination, this work advances our under­ standing of drivers’ gaze strategies around automation transitions and the underlying gaze control causations. These findings provide

Accident Analysis and Prevention 180 (2023) 106905
important implications for the design of autonomous vehicles, particu­ larly in terms of how to ensure a safe and smooth take-over control by guiding drivers’ attention appropriately according to their attentional state and the take-over urgency.
There are also some limitations in the present study. First, there was no take-over driving condition where the HUD was absent, which hin­ dered the authors from examining the effect of the HUD directly from the performed experiments. However, by comparing our results with Louw et al., 2019′s, the effects of the HUD were addressed, which were also explained theoretically based on the gaze control model. Second, the small sample size in the present study hindered the authors from further examining the effect of driver characteristics such as age and experience. Finally, this study has not considered the effects of traffic conditions on drivers’ gaze patterns, such as traffic density and road geometry. However, these factors have been proven to have a significant influence on take-over performance (Gold et al., 2016; Louw et al., 2019). Further research is required to systematically address whether and how variations in driver characteristics and traffic conditions will interfere gaze patterns during take-over driving.
